## 4. Blocking findings (round 2)

### B1 — red evidence does not exist in the form the officina defines

`check studio --repo .` reports, blocking, on this opus's own front matter:

  opus.red_evidence  opera/W-028.md  missing red log(s) for behaviour(s) 1, 2, 3, 4, 5

The contract (checkRedEvidence, packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.ts) is
`ci/reds/<id>/NN.log` with a `# behaviour: / # command: / # exit:` header, as
`bisellium red` writes it — see ci/reds/W-020/01.log for a conforming example.
ci/reds/W-028/ instead holds close-bugs-red.log, close-bugs-green.log,
review-tier-red.log, review-tier-green.log, check-real-officina.log: raw captured
stdout with a trailing bare `exit=N`, no headers, no behaviour numbers, none
produced by `bisellium red`. CLAUDE.md lists `ci/*.log` under "Never by hand";
the standing rule is "evidence is produced, never backfilled". A gate cannot be
waved through on evidence that the officina's own rule scores as absent.

### B2 — the non-conforming naming hides the one weak red from the rule built to catch it

review-tier-red.log is a module-load failure, not an assertion failure:

  SyntaxError: The requested module './process.js' does not provide an export
  named 'reviewTierAdvisory'

Verified directly against the rule's own predicate:

  isModuleLoadFailure(read "studio/ci/reds/W-028/review-tier-red.log") === true

`opus.red_content` (P-005 part 2, W-022's rule) would flag exactly this — but it
only reads `ci/reds/<id>/NN.log`, so a file named review-tier-red.log is invisible
to it. The off-contract name does not merely fail to satisfy the red rule; it
routes around the weak-red rule as well. That red proves the import line was
wrong, not that the behaviour was missing.

### B3 — the brief no longer describes the delivered work

briefs/W-028.md declares 5 behaviours (countBehaviours == 5, all checkpointStale)
and owns three files: rules/process.ts, rules/process.test.ts, briefs/W-028.md.
Delivered under this opus: close.ts (13 behaviours), close.test.ts, the `close`
wiring in main.ts, and reviewTierAdvisory (4 more behaviours, 18-21). None of
those files or behaviours appear in the brief. The spec gate
(probationes.spec, evidence briefs/W-028.md, status passed) therefore certifies a
document that covers roughly a quarter of the opus, and red evidence can never be
reconciled while the brief's numbered list stops at 5. The brief has to be amended
to name the close deliverable and its behaviours before reds can be produced
against it. (Note also that the QA lex §2 cites `(check: probatio.declared)` for
writes outside an opus's declared files; no such rule is implemented, so nothing
caught this mechanically.)

## 5. Advisory findings (round 2, none blocking on their own)

A1. `rebuildDossier` (close.ts:46-48) returns `true` when build.sh is absent, so
    `close --repo <anything without docs/design/dossier/build.sh>` prints
    "<id> closed — dossier rebuilt" and exits 0 having rebuilt nothing — see the
    W-505 transcript in §2. `--repo` defaults to ".", so running close from any
    cwd other than the repo root silently produces this. Same claim-without-doing
    class as round-1 bug 1, one degree milder. Suggest distinguishing "no dossier
    in this repo" from "rebuilt".

A2. `rebuildDossier` captures stdout/stderr (`encoding: "utf8"`) and discards both.
    The fixture script's "boom" never reaches the operator; the failure message
    says only "run build.sh manually". One line: print `r.stderr` on failure.
    (spawnSync's null status on timeout/spawn failure correctly yields false —
    the rebuild fails closed. Good.)

A3. close.ts:31 hardcodes the state list in its error message instead of deriving
    it from CLOSEABLE. Under mutation A the message read "must be building,
    verifying, or review" while the set was {building, reviewing, greenlit} — the
    error lied about the code that produced it. `[...CLOSEABLE].join(", ")` fixes it.

A4. CLOSEABLE duplicates DONE_FROM_STATES with only a comment binding them, and
    DONE_FROM_STATES is not exported. No test asserts the two agree, so the exact
    drift round 1 caught would go undetected again. Exporting DONE_FROM_STATES and
    importing it, or one test asserting set equality, closes the class rather than
    the instance.

A5. process.review_tier checks one declaration against another, not against what
    ran. Its own commit message cites the round-1 incident (review executed at
    Sonnet tier), yet on that very opus it is silent: opera/W-028.md records
    `review: { sella: eng-lead }`, and bisellium.yml binds eng-lead to
    claude-opus-5 — while round 1's log names its reviewer as qa-lead and it in
    fact ran on the censor agent at Sonnet 5. Further, `censor` is not a declared
    sella in studio/bisellium.yml at all, so any gate recorded as `censor` is
    silent by construction ("can't verify, won't guess"). The rule produces zero
    findings on the live officina today. It is correct as written and its doc
    comment is honest about the silence; the gap is that D-014 is about the model
    that executed the gate, and nothing records that. A `model:` field written by
    `review` itself would be checkable; a sella id is not.

A6. The dossier progress rows still carry the inaccurate claim round 1 flagged:
    docs/design/dossier/progress-body.html:32 and
    docs/design/dossier/bisellium-progress.html:300 both read "executeClose
    orchestrates validation + done gate check + dossier rebuild. 3 new behaviours
    (7 total)". executeClose does not rebuild the dossier (only runClose does), and
    close.test.ts now has 13 behaviours. The builder's refusal to move
    rebuildDossier into executeClose is sound — executeClose has exactly one
    non-test caller (runClose, close.ts:74), takes no repo argument, and moving it
    would widen a signature for a caller that does not exist. The commit message
    for 19f2353 is immutable history; these two HTML lines are not, and they are
    the artifact the Patron republishes.

## 6. Verdict

FAIL — on evidence, not on the close fixes.

Both round-1 bugs are genuinely and completely fixed: verified live against the
built CLI on every legitimate entry state, and verified to be under test by
mutation. process.review_tier is correct within its stated scope and its tests
pass. Nothing found in round 2 contradicts that.

What blocks the gate is B1-B3. The opus's own officina reports
opus.red_evidence missing for all five declared behaviours; the logs standing in
for reds were assembled by hand rather than produced by `bisellium red`, contrary
to CLAUDE.md and the standing rules; the naming that puts them off-contract also
hides a module-load red from `opus.red_content`, the rule W-022 exists to enforce;
and the brief still describes a quarter of the shipped work, so there is nothing
to produce conforming reds against until it is amended.

To pass: amend briefs/W-028.md to declare the close deliverable and its
behaviours, produce reds with `bisellium red <opus> --behaviour <n> -- <cmd>` for
every numbered behaviour (a genuine assertion-level red per behaviour, not a
module-load error), re-record the tests/lint/types gates against the current tree,
and re-submit. A3 and A4 are one-line changes worth taking in the same pass; A1,
A2, A5 and A6 are the Censor's advisories, not conditions of the gate.
